## The idea

Use **backtracking** and only ever pick factors in **non-decreasing order**. That way [2, 6] and [6, 2] cannot both appear, so we never need to remove duplicates. At each step try every factor f from the last one used up to √(what is left): f × (n / f) is one complete answer, and we can also keep splitting n / f further.

## Step by step

1. n < 4 → no combinations.
2. Build(remaining, from, path): for f = from while f × f ≤ remaining:
3. If f divides remaining: add f to the path, record path + [remaining / f] as an answer, then call Build(remaining / f, f, path) to split further, then remove f again (backtrack).

## Worked example

**Input:** n = 12

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| f = 2 | 12 = 2 × 6 → record [2, 6]; split 6 further from 2 |
| 6, f = 2 | 6 = 2 × 3 → record [2, 2, 3]; 3 cannot be split (2 × 2 > 3) |
| f = 3 | 12 = 3 × 4 → record [3, 4]; 4 cannot be split from 3 (3 × 3 > 4) |
| f = 4 | 4 × 4 > 12 → stop |

**Result:** [[2, 6], [2, 2, 3], [3, 4]]

## Cost

Proportional to the number of combinations produced (small in practice), each step trying factors up to √n. Recursion depth O(log n).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public static class Factors {

    public static List<List<int>> Combinations(int n) {
        List<List<int>> results = new List<List<int>>();
        if (n < 4) return results;
        Build(n, 2, new List<int>(), results);
        return results;
    }

    // Factors in non-decreasing order, so every combination appears exactly once.
    private static void Build(int remaining, int from, List<int> path, List<List<int>> results) {
        for (int f = from; (long)f * f <= remaining; f++) {     // long: f*f must not overflow
            if (remaining % f != 0) continue;

            path.Add(f);

            List<int> answer = new List<int>(path);              // f × (remaining / f) is complete
            answer.Add(remaining / f);
            results.Add(answer);

            Build(remaining / f, f, path, results);              // or keep splitting remaining / f

            path.RemoveAt(path.Count - 1);                        // backtrack
        }
    }
}
```
